{"black_box_warning":{"current_value":"Current populated LAMICTAL serious skin rash boxed warning text, including pediatric/adult serious rash incidence, valproate/dose-escalation risk factors, HLA-B*1502, and discontinuation-at-first-rash language.","fda_source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/020241s068s069%2C020764s061s062%2C022251s032s033lbl.pdf"],"proposed_value":"No text change required; replace the source metadata with an FDA/accessdata or FDA/openFDA source, not DailyMed.","rationale":"The boxed-warning text is concordant with the FDA-accessdata 10/2025 LAMICTAL label. The current source field cites DailyMed, which is not permissible for this audit's black-box-warning field.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified"},"blocking_questions":[],"fact_checks":[{"current_value":"lamotrigine","field":"generic_name","proposed_value":"lamotrigine","rationale":"FDA labeling identifies LAMICTAL/LAMICTAL ODT/LAMICTAL XR as lamotrigine products.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/020241s068s069%2C020764s061s062%2C022251s032s033lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/022115s031s032lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"","field":"alternate_generic_names","proposed_value":"","rationale":"No separate alternate generic name requiring a separate row was found; brand and abbreviation aliases should be handled in trade_names or pubmed_search_aliases.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/020241s068s069%2C020764s061s062%2C022251s032s033lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Lamictal; Lamictal CD; Lamictal ODT; Lamictal XR; Logem","field":"trade_names","proposed_value":"No automatic change. FDA sources verify Lamictal, Lamictal ODT, and Lamictal XR; add trusted-source support for Lamictal CD and Logem, or user-approve narrowing to: Lamictal; Lamictal ODT; Lamictal XR","rationale":"Lamictal/ODT/XR are supported by FDA labeling. I could not verify Lamictal CD and Logem from the trusted domains available in this audit; Lamotrigine should not be added as a trade name because it is the generic name.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"insufficient_evidence","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/020241s068s069%2C020764s061s062%2C022251s032s033lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/022115s031s032lbl.pdf","https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/8052/smpc"]},{"current_value":"","field":"pubmed_search_aliases","proposed_value":"LTG; Lamictal","rationale":"LTG and Lamictal appear in lamotrigine trial literature and would improve alias capture without creating duplicate drug rows.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"missing","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10403222/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2498073/"]},{"current_value":"Yes","field":"available_in_us","proposed_value":"Yes","rationale":"FDA labeling and Drugs@FDA records support current US availability/approval history for Lamictal products.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/020241s068s069%2C020764s061s062%2C022251s032s033lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/nda/2009/022251_lamictal_odt_lamotrigine.cfm"]},{"current_value":"1994","field":"year_fda_cleared","proposed_value":"1994","rationale":"FDA label states Initial U.S. Approval: 1994; deterministic openFDA nonconcordance finding should not override the FDA label/accessdata evidence.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/020241s068s069%2C020764s061s062%2C022251s032s033lbl.pdf","https://www.fda.gov/media/190579/download"]},{"current_value":"CNS: dizziness 38%, CNS: headache 29%, ophthalmologic: diplopia 28%, neurologic: ataxia 22%, GI: nausea 19%, dermatologic: rash 10%","field":"adverse_symptoms_percentages","proposed_value":"CNS: dizziness 38%, CNS: headache 29%, ophthalmologic: diplopia 28%, neurologic: ataxia 22%, GI: nausea 19%, dermatologic: rash 10%","rationale":"FDA Table 8 for pooled placebo-controlled adjunctive adult epilepsy trials reports these exact lamotrigine-arm percentages.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/020241s068s069%2C020764s061s062%2C022251s032s033lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Focal; Generalized; Absence; LGS; Generalized tonic-clonic","field":"epilepsy_type","proposed_value":"Focal; Generalized; Absence; LGS; Generalized tonic-clonic","rationale":"FDA label supports partial/focal, PGTC, and LGS; eMC/PubMed evidence supports typical absence use/evidence.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/020241s068s069%2C020764s061s062%2C022251s032s033lbl.pdf","https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/2567/smpc","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10403222/"]},{"current_value":"Tablet; chewable/dispersible tablet; orally disintegrating tablet; extended-release tablet","field":"formulations_available","proposed_value":"Tablet; chewable/dispersible/tablet for oral suspension; orally disintegrating tablet; extended-release tablet","rationale":"FDA label supports tablets, tablets for oral suspension that may be chewed/dispersed, ODT, and XR tablets. The current wording is acceptable but can be made more US-label-concordant.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/020241s068s069%2C020764s061s062%2C022251s032s033lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/022115s031s032lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Not a CYP inducer/inhibitor; glucuronidation substrate","field":"enzyme_inducing_or_inhibiting","proposed_value":"Not a clinically meaningful CYP inducer/inhibitor; primarily metabolized by UGT glucuronidation and affected by glucuronidation inducers/inhibitors","rationale":"FDA/eMC sources identify UGT/glucuronidation metabolism, valproate inhibition, and enzyme-inducer effects; eMC notes CYP-mediated interactions are unlikely.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/020241s068s069%2C020764s061s062%2C022251s032s033lbl.pdf","https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/2567/smpc"]},{"current_value":"25-33 h; shorter with enzyme inducers, longer with valproate","field":"half_life_range","proposed_value":"25-33 h without interacting drugs; shorter with enzyme inducers, longer with valproate","rationale":"FDA pharmacokinetic table reports about 25.4 h after multiple dosing and 32.8 h after single dosing without interacting drugs, with shorter half-life on inducers and longer with valproate.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/020241s068s069%2C020764s061s062%2C022251s032s033lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Liver","field":"major_organ_for_metabolism","proposed_value":"Liver/hepatic glucuronidation","rationale":"Metabolism is primarily by glucuronidation; labeling includes hepatic impairment dose adjustment. The current value is directionally correct but less specific.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/020241s068s069%2C020764s061s062%2C022251s032s033lbl.pdf","https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/2567/smpc"]},{"current_value":"Up to 500 mg/day for adjunctive therapy depending on interacting ASMs","field":"maximum_approved_daily_dose","proposed_value":"Up to 500 mg/day for immediate-release adjunctive epilepsy depending on interacting ASMs; up to 600 mg/day for Lamictal XR adjunctive therapy depending on concomitant AEDs","rationale":"Immediate-release dosing supports up to 500 mg/day in some adjunctive regimens; FDA XR labeling lists an adjunctive target therapeutic range up to 600 mg/day.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/020241s068s069%2C020764s061s062%2C022251s032s033lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/022115s031s032lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Maintenance depends on interacting ASMs; adult effective maintenance commonly 100-500 mg/day","field":"minimum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"Maintenance depends on interacting ASMs; adult immediate-release maintenance commonly spans 100-500 mg/day by regimen","rationale":"FDA dosing tables support broad adult maintenance ranges dependent on valproate/enzyme-inducer status.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/020241s068s069%2C020764s061s062%2C022251s032s033lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Dose depends strongly on valproate/enzyme inducers; adults commonly 100-500 mg/day","field":"typical_doses_per_day","proposed_value":"Dose depends strongly on valproate/enzyme inducers; adults commonly 100-500 mg/day for immediate-release regimens","rationale":"Consistent with FDA dosing tables for adult epilepsy regimens.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/020241s068s069%2C020764s061s062%2C022251s032s033lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Precise anticonvulsant mechanism is unknown; a proposed mechanism is inhibition of voltage-sensitive sodium channels with reduced glutamate and aspartate release.","field":"mechanism_of_action","proposed_value":"Precise anticonvulsant mechanism is unknown; a proposed mechanism is inhibition of voltage-sensitive sodium channels with reduced glutamate and aspartate release.","rationale":"FDA clinical pharmacology section supports the proposed sodium-channel effect and reduced excitatory amino acid release, while stating human relevance remains to be established.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/020241s068s069%2C020764s061s062%2C022251s032s033lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Moderate","field":"mechanism_confidence","proposed_value":"Moderate","rationale":"Appropriate because the label describes a proposed mechanism and states its human relevance remains to be established.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/020241s068s069%2C020764s061s062%2C022251s032s033lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed labeling; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review","field":"mechanism_source","proposed_value":"FDA/accessdata labeling; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review","rationale":"Use FDA/accessdata rather than DailyMed as the named FDA-label source.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/020241s068s069%2C020764s061s062%2C022251s032s033lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"FDA label","field":"mechanism_source_tier","proposed_value":"FDA label","rationale":"FDA label directly supports the mechanism statement.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/020241s068s069%2C020764s061s062%2C022251s032s033lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"No clinically meaningful QT prolongation; avoid in certain cardiac conduction/structural disease per warning","field":"qt_interval_effect","proposed_value":"No clinically meaningful QT prolongation; conduction/arrhythmia caution in clinically important structural or functional heart disease","rationale":"FDA label says lamotrigine did not slow ventricular conduction in healthy individuals in a thorough QT study, but may slow ventricular conduction and induce proarrhythmia in certain cardiac disease settings.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/020241s068s069%2C020764s061s062%2C022251s032s033lbl.pdf","https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-safety-and-availability/studies-show-increased-risk-heart-rhythm-problems-seizure-and-mental-health-medicine-lamotrigine"]},{"current_value":"Glutamate receptor","field":"filter_mechanism","proposed_value":"Sodium channel; Reduced excitatory amino acid release","rationale":"The label supports voltage-sensitive sodium-channel inhibition with modulation of glutamate/aspartate release, and specifically reports no NMDA receptor inhibition at tested concentrations. A glutamate receptor filter is misleading.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/020241s068s069%2C020764s061s062%2C022251s032s033lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Conduction/arrhythmia caution; No known meaningful QT effect; QT prolongation","field":"filter_qt_effect","proposed_value":"Conduction/arrhythmia caution; No known meaningful QT effect","rationale":"FDA evidence supports conduction/arrhythmia caution, not QT prolongation as a clinically meaningful effect.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/020241s068s069%2C020764s061s062%2C022251s032s033lbl.pdf","https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-safety-and-availability/studies-show-increased-risk-heart-rhythm-problems-seizure-and-mental-health-medicine-lamotrigine"]},{"current_value":"NCBI LiverTox anticonvulsants table; Epilepsy Society ASM list; Epilepsy Foundation Australia ASM list; FDA/DailyMed labeling; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review","field":"evidence_sources","proposed_value":"FDA/accessdata labeling; eMC SmPC; NCBI/PubMed reviews; PubMed randomized placebo-controlled trial reports; ClinicalTrials.gov trial records; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review","rationale":"Current evidence_sources includes non-trusted-domain sources for this audit and cites DailyMed. FDA/accessdata, eMC, PubMed/NCBI, and ClinicalTrials.gov better match the row-source policy.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"missing_source","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/020241s068s069%2C020764s061s062%2C022251s032s033lbl.pdf","https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/8052/smpc","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10712213/","https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT00104416","https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT00113165"]},{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed SPL; status=boxed_warning_found; setid=d7e3572d-56fe-4727-2bb4-013ccca22678; published=Nov 17, 2025; title=LAMICTAL...; url=https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=d7e3572d-56fe-4727-2bb4-013ccca22678","field":"fda_black_box_warning_source","proposed_value":"FDA/accessdata drug label; status=boxed_warning_found; application=NDA 020241/020764/022251; revised=10/2025; reference_id=5675542; title=LAMICTAL (lamotrigine) tablets/tablets for oral suspension/LAMICTAL ODT; url=https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/020241s068s069%2C020764s061s062%2C022251s032s033lbl.pdf","rationale":"DailyMed is not permissible for FDA black-box-warning verification under the audit rules; FDA/accessdata is permissible and supports the warning.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/020241s068s069%2C020764s061s062%2C022251s032s033lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Current broad-use ASM; also used for bipolar disorder maintenance.","field":"status_or_notes","proposed_value":"Current broad-use ASM; FDA-approved for epilepsy indications and bipolar I maintenance treatment.","rationale":"FDA label supports epilepsy indications and bipolar I maintenance treatment; 'broad-use' is reasonable but less directly source-specific.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/020241s068s069%2C020764s061s062%2C022251s032s033lbl.pdf"]}],"generic_name":"lamotrigine","missing_rcts":[{"pmid":"8232944","proposed_link_text":"Matsuo1993|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8232944/","title":"Placebo-controlled study of the efficacy and safety of lamotrigine in patients with partial seizures. U.S. Lamotrigine Protocol 0.5 Clinical Trial Group.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8232944/","why_relevant":"Multicenter randomized double-blind parallel-group placebo-controlled adjunctive lamotrigine trial in refractory partial seizures; FDA label describes n=216 and median seizure-frequency reductions of 8% placebo, 20% at 300 mg/day, and 36% at 500 mg/day."},{"pmid":"18077797","proposed_link_text":"PinaGarza2007|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18077797/","title":"Adjunctive lamotrigine for partial seizures in patients aged 1 to 24 months.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18077797/","why_relevant":"Randomized double-blind placebo-controlled infant partial-seizure trial; relevant as a negative/young-pediatric study if the CSV retains negative placebo-controlled lamotrigine epilepsy trials."}],"outcome_checks":[{"current_value":"17-23.2 % (Naritoku2007 23.2%; Biton2005 23%; Motte1997 17%; Schapel1993 22%)","field":"diff_50_responder_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"17-23.2 % (drug minus placebo RR50 differential at maximum effective dose/regimen: Naritoku2007 lamotrigine XR individualized adjunctive dose 23.2%; Biton2005 lamotrigine individualized adjunctive dose 23%; Motte1997 lamotrigine individualized adjunctive dose 17%; Schapel1993 lamotrigine 150-300 mg/day 22%)","rationale":"Cited values match extractable active-m